{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc 'cat /tmp/research_history/episode_5_continuing_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": "# Continuing research notes: episode 5 complete\n\n## Best fully evaluated state\n- Best: **265_mean_skip3_linear_column**, raw **0.04625840150013876 seconds**, score **0.9112964935233014**, all 30 quality-valid.\n- Exact evaluator source hash: **cbb503193aabdeefdcb35fa0d64660e7ed23e9237f69481998f6c2c3ca1dbece**.\n- Worst nRMSE **0.00788923531667515**, minimum Spearman **0.9999780382197817**, minimum top-quarter overlap **0.996**. All 270 quality records valid.\n- Current method.py is restored to the exact measured best. No evaluation pending. Only method.py and frozen solve.sh are root submission files; generated method cache removed.\n- Episode start source162 official repeat172: raw0.05629953476842881, score0.892041964594227, same source hashf57def419ac069782eeb981cefb9157e8d2a80d927c6794c0cd116203a4b37a6. Best raw is **17.84%** lower than that repeat. Prior episode162 measured0.0648742; timing drift is substantial.\n- Frozen solve.sh SHA256 **bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1**. Run `sh ./solve.sh`.\n\n## Official results\n| Variant | Raw seconds | Score | Quality valid | Exact evaluator source hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|\n| 172_start162_repeat | 0.05629953476842881 | 0.892041964594227 | True | f57def419ac069782eeb981cefb9157e8d2a80d927c6794c0cd116203a4b37a6 |\n| 190_fuse_direct_both | 0.062160325032891706 | 0.8808035164351224 | True | 60489b287ca6e1ef56ce61b31413985781b0b1ec5db15861090abae9d62f92c4 |\n| 224_mean_initial | 0.06020816953338605 | 0.8845469021202808 | True | 173297cfd168e6e336844f253f9baadbd31c77a8ecd2f627a584de962ef32b44 |\n| 241_numpy_label_take | 0.049951216733218944 | 0.9042152790990676 | True | d26af51d56550306674075a9be4114694acf97d6f835602aebeaa9139587b25b |\n| 264_mean_hard16_linear_column | 0.0488753250654554 | 0.9062783716492805 | True | 530ce257edf08b325935c8d67349714134490d5e92d12f438f63a6528f41a725 |\n| 265_mean_skip3_linear_column | 0.04625840150013876 | 0.9112964935233014 | True | cbb503193aabdeefdcb35fa0d64660e7ed23e9237f69481998f6c2c3ca1dbece |\n\nAll original reports and evaluator logs are preserved unchanged in the respective history directories. Retention uses complete official quality and raw results. Local screens compare against values computed from the saved canonical initial implementation using public arrays; they are not evaluator scores.\n\n## Current method\nThe retained source keeps all empirical class supports and contiguous batches of at most 1024. Class geometry uses 100 padded cross-class transport problems from the first candidate/reference batches. Feature costs use precomputed squared norms and matrix products, then add empirical label cost divided by 2. Point potentials are calibrated by subtracting their batch mean and multiplying by n/(n-1). Outer coupling uses the safeguarded scalar Newton solve for two reference batches, with canonical POT fallback otherwise.\n\nThe first point soft transform is approximated by mean costs. The residual exponential kernels use reciprocal row/column factors and two squares when temperature quarters, with stable direct fallback. Direct exponent scaling constants are fused. Class potentials initialize analytically from feature means and norms; later class transforms still use all empirical supports. Label costs are gathered with NumPy np.take from contiguous class rows, avoiding two-dimensional Torch advanced indexing. The second coupling column balancing factor uses the first-order reciprocal approximation 2-t at t=m*column_mass; the first balancing update and the final row balance are exact. No input caches, learned parameters, lookup outputs, solver I/O, private material or corruption truth are used.\n\nThis retained265 variant uses point schedule[3:-3], omitting one more coarse update than241, and class hard transforms at eps<=8. It retains both point dual transforms in each update.\n\n## Hypotheses, ablations and decisions\n- 173-175 group class pairs by support size into2/4/10 working sets, preserving original schedules and simultaneous hard-transform switch. All 30 valid, but dispatch/copy overhead offsets smaller padding; not retained.\n- 176-179 store point/class kernels in float16 or bfloat16. Broad point half fails10 cases; broad bfloat fails all 30 with large errors. Half class passes but is slower.255/256 defer point reduced precision until eps<=128: both pass, but still slower, especially half due fallback.257-259 balance coupling around unit element means to avoid half underflow; float32/half/bfloat all pass, reduced precision still slower. These failures and slow successes are preserved.\n- 180-183 use structured degree1/2 feature moments for early point transforms, retaining exact radial/label factors. First two transformations pass; extending degree2 to three or four fails.198 cubic first three passes but costs more.199/200 initial-only moments pass.224 mean initialization and225 mean-minus-variance initialization pass; mean is cheapest.230 analytical point means avoid dense reduction but do not improve fresh timings.231/232 remove one further coarse point update, both pass.236/237 analytical class mean initialization passes with low error.238/239 earlier class hard threshold16/32 passes; the two cutoffs give identical public outputs.\n- 184-187 remove1/2/3/4 late class updates; first three pass, four fails10.233/234 combine this with point means.188-190 fuse exponent constants and remove negligible tiny-tail clamping/redundant padding masks; all pass, but official190 is slower than the starting-source repeat.201/235 eliminate the unused last candidate dual, with235 preserving the kernel recurrence and column-only stable fallback.\n- 202-205 subsample class support by strides2/3, optionally preserving means; all 30 fail even with mean correction.206-210 deterministic weighted barycenters compress classes to64/32/16 centers.64 without variance correction passes; variance corrections and smaller supports generally fail.211 full-support weighted control matches the original.217-223 weighted reduction and moment combinations pass at64/80;48 fails9.226-229 use NumPy or batched Torch clustering to reduce setup overhead, but fresh medians still give no speed benefit.246/247 with label take also pass but are slower than simpler methods. Reject barycenter compression for retention.\n- 212-216 replace Torch reductions with matrix-vector/batched matrix-vector products and hybrid coupling contractions. All pass but matrix-vector versions are slower.250-252 one-thread controls on the new workloads pass but are slower.\n- 240-243 test label gathering with NumPy advanced indices, contiguous np.take, cached Torch indices and SciPy feature geometry. All pass.241 np.take is the largest measured gain; SciPy geometry is slower.260 two Torch index_select operations and261 a global label matrix match outputs but show no fresh speed gain over np.take.\n- 244-249 combine class initialization, last-column updates, earlier hard transforms and label take. All pass.253/254 linearize the second coupling column or both column/row factors; both pass.253 reduces worst local error to0.0035153.262-265 combine this with the class/point improvements; all pass, and264/265 received complete official evaluation.\n\n## Fresh measurements and artifacts\nFive isolated fresh-process benchmark rounds used8 rounds per variant in alternating order, on public303_gaussian016. Final round medians:241 .0441859,249 .0435620,254 .0444583,262 .0424352,263 .0413659,264 .0413984,265 .0400741. Official results determine retention; these diagnostics selected promising candidates.\n\nEpisode 5 preserved **94 source snapshots172-265**, **93 complete local screens**, **78 all 30 local-valid attempts**, **15 attempts with numerical failures**, and **6 complete official evaluations**. All sources, builders, screen logs, fresh timing logs, exact reports and source hashes remain under `/tmp/research_history`. `episode_5_index.json` and `episode_5_final_selection.json` summarize them. `episode_5_working_notes.md` records chronology. Earlier episode notes remain preserved.\n\n## Continuation\nStart from the exact retained source and profile it before changing the next bottleneck. Matrix construction, point kernel scaling/reductions and the remaining padded class operations are likely the next limits. General grouping, lower precision, class subsampling, weighted barycenters and structured polynomial coupling have been explored and did not improve retained official performance. A cheaper fused reduction implementation or geometry reuse may help, but preserve the canonical finite annealing recipe and measure the full protocol. Prior episode4 Taylor coupling degrees1-4 were too inaccurate or expensive; do not repeat without a materially different approximation.\n\nNo protected assets or frozen launcher changed. No protected targets, private cases, evaluator source, other trajectories, credentials, environment variables, network or subagents accessed. Only public arrays, local canonical calculations, and official report outputs were used.\n\nFinal diagnostic profile (public303_gaussian016, all inputs loaded before timing):0.061117 seconds including profiling overhead; class solves0.027, point duals0.018, coupling0.007, NumPy label take0.002. This is not an evaluator score. Remaining class direct transforms and kernel broadcasts/reductions dominate. No evaluation pending.\n# Continuing research notes: episode 4 complete\n\n## Best fully evaluated state\n- Best is **162_precomputed_norm_geometry**, raw **0.06487423613192125 seconds**, score **0.8755994146299784**, all 30 quality-valid.\n- Exact evaluator source hash: **f57def419ac069782eeb981cefb9157e8d2a80d927c6794c0cd116203a4b37a6**. Worst nRMSE 0.0039617933199789625, minimum Spearman 0.9999831555536839, minimum top-quarter overlap 0.996.\n- **Current method.py is restored to the exact best source. No evaluation pending.** Only method.py and frozen solve.sh are root submission files. Verify profile/cleanup completion in episode_4_working_notes.md.\n- Starting best123 raw .07845975276820051. Best measured raw improved 17.32% in episode 4.\n- Frozen launcher SHA256 bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1. Use `sh ./solve.sh`.\n\n## Official results\n| Variant | Raw seconds | Score | All cases valid | Exact evaluator source hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|\n| 136_all_torch_reductions | 0.0735952235995986 | 0.8588763515055424 | True | f6fb552bc2afd0b84bb2daa3a8d912d159992a8d9604b98d8f396fa402bfe60c |\n| 145_inplace_matrix_scaling | 0.06843345973272032 | 0.8687743708868532 | True | 1da84d688b4a1dde5586eb23b05e48769639b4f0b56931b8bbb9d6a92b1a6492 |\n| 162_precomputed_norm_geometry | 0.06487423613192125 | 0.8755994146299784 | True | f57def419ac069782eeb981cefb9157e8d2a80d927c6794c0cd116203a4b37a6 |\n| 165_addmm_baddbmm_geometry | 0.07197932919904512 | 0.8619749345688746 | True | 45380e25239e9c12ec4828fcf80f9314d981ca792c4d485523e4e7849f6bcae7 |\n\nAll original result reports and evaluator logs are preserved unchanged in the corresponding history directories. Retention uses actual complete official raw results, not local screens or self-reported scores. Timing noise remains substantial. Formal baseline B=.5214946210980997 was inferred from the evaluator's score formula in episode 3; do not infer its sample SD from earlier local baseline runs.\n\n## Current numerical method\n- Same contiguous 1024 batches and 100 empirical cross-class transport costs from the first candidate/reference batches. Float32 geometry is shared by point potentials and coupling. No output lookup, learned constants, persistent input cache, or solver I/O.\n- Retains best123's shared residual exponential kernel recurrence: reciprocal row/column factors, two squares when temperature quarters, scalar shift bookkeeping. Kernel maximum normalization is omitted; relative positive-mass checks trigger stable direct fallback. Mask class padding to zero.\n- Initial full soft transform, two duplicate coarse symmetric updates skipped; three late point updates removed. Class hard minima only when all eps<=8; point hard minima at eps<=2; final candidate transform uses hard min at .1. This finite annealed recipe approximates the canonical finite schedule, not a fully converged alternative objective.\n- Coupling now normalizes the dense kernel's columns to 1/m and rows to 1/n, twice, then sums K*C. Algebraically equivalent to two alternating Sinkhorn vector scaling steps up to float32 rounding; removes transpose copies and matrix-vector overhead. Outer entropy regularization remains .01 after batch-cost max normalization. For two reference batches, safeguarded scalar Newton solves the balancing condition; other counts use canonical POT.\n- Vectorize all 100 class bounding boxes and repeated padded feature assignments. Compute feature squared norms once and use direct squared-distance matrix products. Add W/2 to point costs.\n- Reuse final cost buffers by subtracting point g or class f,g only after their last other use. Supplied feature arrays are never modified.\n- Direct dense exponentials use exp2(z*log2(e)). Two Torch threads, flush-denormal flag set before thread initialization.\n-162 uses ordinary matrix products plus in-place feature norm additions;165 uses addmm feature and baddbmm class products. See the best source identified above for which implementation was retained.\n\n## Experiments and decisions\n-126-131: vectorized class setup, first-distance sharing, global distance sharing, packed class geometry, in-place coupling energy/mass. All 30 local-valid, worst nRMSE about .0039615. Sharing geometry is accurate; gather/copy costs offset some savings.\n-132: one coupling update failed six cases (worst .0117195863); reject.133-136: initial column sum, strided transpose, weighted column reduction, all Torch reductions. All 30 valid.136 improved the full official result.\n-137-139: NumPy point/class/both recurrences passed all 30 but were slower.140: unpadded class blocks via sorting and NumPy reduceat passed all 30, but fresh median .16799 versus136 .07630; reject for speed. All supports retained.\n-141-146: global geometry, vectorized setup, pow4, exp2, in-place coupling balance, streamed hard reductions. All valid. Single pow4 much slower than two squares.145 became the next measured best. Streamed hard transforms had little effect because coarse point schedules rarely enter that branch.\n-147-156: separate/both final-buffer mutation, streamed final point transform, combinations of geometry sharing, matrix scaling, exp2. All valid; local speed changes were noisy.\n-157-160: NumPy einsum fused kernel broadcast scaling and fused first square. All valid, but slower, especially square fusion (.20-.24 seconds versus dense .07); reject.\n-161-165: combined vectorized setup, matrix scaling, final buffer reuse and exp2; precomputed feature norms; addmm features; baddbmm classes; both fused geometries. All local 30-valid. Fresh round4 medians:145 .07841,151 .06501,153 .07443,155 .06752,161 .06829,162 .06322,163 .06543,165 .06457.162 and165 received complete official evaluations; choose by reported raw above.\n-166-168: structured Taylor expansion of exp(centered feature dot/beta) to degree1/2/3 with exact radial and empirical label factors. Grouped polynomial moments replace dense kernel applications and energy contraction; all supports retained. Fail30/27/19 cases, worst nRMSE .04324/.03411/.01691.\n-169-170: class-wise matrix energy contractions accelerate degree2/3 Taylor variants but preserve the same failures.171 degree4 passes27/30, worst .0110019202, local mean .28121. Reject structured polynomial compression: insufficient accuracy at cheap degrees, slower than dense coupling at higher degrees.\n\n## Research artifacts and continuation\n-46 new source attempts126-171,39 local all-30 valid,7 with quality failures,4 complete official evaluations. Sources, builders, local logs, fresh-process timing logs, exact reports and hashes preserved under /tmp/research_history. `episode_4_index.json` is the machine-readable summary; `episode_4_working_notes.md` records chronology. Prior episode notes are preserved.\n-`screen_all.py` compares public arrays against outputs computed by the saved canonical initial source, not protected targets. These screens are not scores. `fresh_bench.py` runs eight fresh processes per variant in alternating order. First paired round1 overlapped a local screen and is marked diagnostic; subsequent fresh rounds had no concurrent numerical work. Official evaluations had no concurrent numerical workloads.\n-Only public inputs and local canonical calculations used. No protected targets, evaluator source, private inputs, other trajectories, credentials, environment variables, network or subagents accessed. No protected assets or frozen launcher changed.\n-For a fresh episode, start from the exact measured best. Profile it before choosing the next bottleneck. Remaining point-dual kernel recurrence and class symmetric transforms likely dominate. Possible next hypothesis: group class pairs by support size while retaining Torch contiguous reductions, reducing padding and dense working sets. The full NumPy segmented solver and Taylor compression were slower; repeating them is unlikely useful. Public nRMSE margin is about .006.\n\nFinal checks completed: exact best162 source and frozen launcher hashes verified; all270 official quality records valid; root submission files exactly method.py and solve.sh. Own generated method pycache removed. Final local profile303_gaussian016 .06931s: point dual .022s, class .021s, coupling .007s. Next focus is class/point reductions and broadcast kernel arithmetic. No evaluation pending.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc 'cat /tmp/research_history/000_baseline/method.py /tmp/research_history/episode_6_continuing_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": "\"\"\"Canonical initial numerical method. All geometry is computed from inputs.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nimport ot\nfrom geomloss import SamplesLoss\nfrom scipy.spatial.distance import cdist\n\ndef _label_cost(x,y,r,ry):\n    xx=torch.tensor(np.concatenate([x,r]),dtype=torch.float32)\n    yy=np.concatenate([y,ry+10])\n    W=np.zeros((20,20),dtype=np.float32)\n    loss=SamplesLoss('sinkhorn',p=2,blur=np.sqrt(.1),debias=False,backend='tensorized')\n    with torch.inference_mode():\n        for i in range(20):\n            for j in range(i+1,20):\n                if not (yy==i).any() or not (yy==j).any():\n                    raise ValueError('Initial batch lacks a label support')\n                W[i,j]=W[j,i]=float(loss(xx[yy==i],xx[yy==j]))\n    return W\n\ndef value(x,y,r,ry,seed=17,batch_size=1024):\n    torch.set_num_threads(2)\n    x=np.asarray(x,np.float32); r=np.asarray(r,np.float32)\n    y=np.asarray(y,np.int64); ry=np.asarray(ry,np.int64)\n    tx=[np.arange(i,min(i+batch_size,len(x))) for i in range(0,len(x),batch_size)]\n    tr=[np.arange(i,min(i+batch_size,len(r))) for i in range(0,len(r),batch_size)]\n    W=_label_cost(x[tx[0]],y[tx[0]],r[tr[0]],ry[tr[0]])\n    def cost(z1,z2):\n        d=torch.cdist(z1[:,:,:-1],z2[:,:,:-1]).square()/2\n        return d+torch.as_tensor(W)[z1[:,:,-1].long().unsqueeze(2),z2[:,:,-1].long().unsqueeze(1)]/2\n    potential=SamplesLoss('sinkhorn',p=2,blur=np.sqrt(.1),debias=False,\n                         potentials=True,cost=cost,backend='tensorized')\n    F={}; batchcost=np.zeros((len(tx),len(tr)))\n    for i,idx in enumerate(tx):\n        for j,jdx in enumerate(tr):\n            a=x[idx];b=r[jdx]\n            C=cdist(a,b,'sqeuclidean')/2+W[y[idx,None],ry[jdx][None,:]+10]/2\n            P=ot.sinkhorn(ot.unif(len(idx)),ot.unif(len(jdx)),C/max(float(C.max()),1e-12),.1)\n            batchcost[i,j]=float(np.sum(P*C))/(len(idx)+len(jdx))\n            z1=torch.tensor(np.column_stack([a,y[idx]]),dtype=torch.float32)[None]\n            z2=torch.tensor(np.column_stack([b,ry[jdx]+10]),dtype=torch.float32)[None]\n            with torch.inference_mode(): f,_=potential(z1,z2)\n            f=f.numpy().ravel().astype(float)\n            F[i,j]=(f-f.mean())*len(f)/(len(f)-1)\n    Q=ot.sinkhorn(ot.unif(len(tx)),ot.unif(len(tr)),\n                  batchcost/max(float(batchcost.max()),1e-12),.01,\n                  numItermax=10000,stopThr=1e-10)\n    residual=max(np.max(np.abs(Q.sum(1)-ot.unif(len(tx)))),\n                 np.max(np.abs(Q.sum(0)-ot.unif(len(tr)))))\n    if residual>1e-6: raise ValueError(f'Unconverged batch transport: {residual}')\n    values=np.zeros(len(x))\n    for i,idx in enumerate(tx): values[idx]=sum(Q[i,j]*F[i,j] for j in range(len(tr)))\n    return values\n# Continuing research notes: episode 6 complete\n\n## Best fully evaluated state\n- Best: **367_simplified_359_row_energy_353_absolute_mass_344_global_coupling_308_combo_hard256_late2_skip2_last1_linear0**, raw **0.04491611540094406 seconds**, score **0.9138704147966757**, all 30 quality-valid.\n- Exact evaluator source hash: **ca9ba4b195760dc34509b9f79d287bb79d1b161478e76c8d53f19b0ef25c2804**.\n- Worst nRMSE **0.007052958964993517**, minimum Spearman **0.9999570711063411**, minimum top-quarter overlap **0.992**. All 270 quality records valid.\n- Current method.py restored to the exact measured best. No evaluation pending.\n- Frozen solve.sh SHA256 **bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1**. Run `sh ./solve.sh`.\n\n## Official results in episode 6\n| Variant | Raw seconds | Score | Quality valid | Exact evaluator source hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|\n| 266_start265_repeat | 0.05008928056631703 | 0.9039505326807684 | True | cbb503193aabdeefdcb35fa0d64660e7ed23e9237f69481998f6c2c3ca1dbece |\n| 303_combo_hard64_late2_skip2_last0 | 0.05072115703320985 | 0.9027388682813114 | True | 3b49deca1907c9ce9ffafc3990eee737f244b09afb283e1bc4ffb929e4ff17a5 |\n| 344_global_coupling_308_combo_hard256_late2_skip2_last1 | 0.04829915573354811 | 0.9073832139786109 | True | 9dffe0a408fc8d8922f8cc936a706c17b159a12f5741a23309ae22fbf1db3db8 |\n| 359_row_energy_353_absolute_mass_344_global_coupling_308_combo_hard256_late2_skip2_last1_linear0 | 0.049546916665470536 | 0.904990550887868 | True | a3db49d67e5ee1817e37a3b14771f73c520f121160e08e2f9c6df656b8313b8d |\n| 367_simplified_359_row_energy_353_absolute_mass_344_global_coupling_308_combo_hard256_late2_skip2_last1_linear0 | 0.04491611540094406 | 0.9138704147966757 | True | ca9ba4b195760dc34509b9f79d287bb79d1b161478e76c8d53f19b0ef25c2804 |\n\nAll original official reports and evaluator logs are preserved unchanged in their source directories. Historical265 (previous episode best) raw0.04625840150013876 was included in final selection. Episode6 start-source repeat266 raw0.05008928056631703 demonstrates substantial timing variation. All retention decisions use complete official quality and raw results, never diagnostic local timings.\n\n## Method and mathematical findings\nThe retained method derives empirical class geometry from all first-batch supports and retains contiguous1024 point batches. The selected source uses class hard transforms at eps<=256, omits two final class refinements, uses in-place class dual updates, and computes the final point iteration only for the needed reference dual. Point annealing remains schedule[3:-3]. Coupling expectation is computed exactly as mean(row_energy/row_mass) after the linearized second column correction. Positive-mass safeguards use1e-34 with stable direct fallback; unreachable full-kernel initialization branches and redundant setup are removed. Read its source for the final combination; do not assume every experiment below is retained. It contains no input caches, learned constants, I/O, lookup outputs, private material, or corruption truth.\n\nClass transport:\n-267/268/269 test NumPy hard refinement, contiguous separate hard transforms, and small in-place dual updates; all pass, fresh speed gains inconsistent.\n-270 degree2 feature moments for one early class update passes. Extending to2/3 updates271/272 fails21/30 cases.295/296 compute the first class residual covariance analytically (agree with dense cumulant286), but covariance setup offsets savings.297 first-order feature moments pass but accuracy margin shrinks.\n-277 solves all100 class pairs on an unpadded sorted global matrix with NumPy reduceat and exact independent annealing. It passes but averages~0.276s, much slower.278 uses cropped NumPy blocks only for hard refinements and also fails to improve speed.\n-273-276/289-291 use fixed reduced-cost class neighborhoods during late hard refinement, retain final dense transform. Top1 at eps<=8 passes; earlier top1 at32 fails3. Union of row and column choices298/299 improves accuracy (union32 passes), but union64 fails25 unless refreshed every2updates301/302. Refresh and scatter overhead erase speed savings.\n-279-282/321 switch to hard class transforms at32/64/128/256/512, all30 pass with current point recipe.1024 fails10. All-hard323-326/328 fails27. Mean initialization cannot replace all high-temperature empirical transport.\n-283 skips one more coarse class update, passes.284 skips two, fails1.303-308 combine earlier hard transforms, truncated late class updates, and point last-column simplification; all pass, official303 slower than baseline repeat.\n-364/365 switch classhard by schedule step rather than temperature; fail26/13, so retain input-scale-sensitive thresholds.\n\nPoint transport:\n-309/310 compute global residual variance densely/analytically and approximate first retained point c-transform with second cumulant. They agree numerically and fail20 cases, maxnRMSE0.02035.311 applies that approximation at one earlier coarser temperature, then resumes retained exact updates, passes with max0.0036294 but costs more.\n-329-333 approximate point soft transforms using exact10-label mixture and conditional feature means/covariances. Pooled within-class covariance329 fails1; full class-specific covariance330 passes all30 max0.00740816, but~0.077smean. Means-only331 fails24. Earlier coarse temperature332/333 passes with~0.00359worsterror but slower. Adaptive dense fallback334-336 all pass and cost more.\n-340-342/347 share batch statistics betweenpairs; accuracy identical and no speed gain.337-339 solve2/3/6 pointpairs together with padded dense kernels; accuracy matches baseline, larger working sets increase time. Reject grouped point solves.\n-292/293 share first feature geometry using np.take/index_select; all valid, no robust fresh speed gain.294 packed class geometry allvalid but slower.\n-349 exact last point column-only update matches retained accuracy.350-355 use absolute positive-mass checks1e-34 in place of relative min/max checks, retain stable direct fallback; allvalid.361-363 skip checks at high temperatures and also preserve reported outputs, no demonstrated retained speed improvement.\n\nTransport weights:\n-313 replaces the final row normalization after the second column correction with a scalar total-mass normalization. All30 pass max0.00972006.314 omits any normalization, fails5.315 linearizes last row factor (passes).316 computes first-order energy correction (passes, slower).317 one Sinkhorn step fails9. Earlier point cumulants318-320 do not make one step universally valid.\n-343-346 combine global normalization with class simplifications. All pass locally. Official344 passes with raw0.04829915573354811 but loses to historical2650.0462584.\n-356 computes exact row-balanced expectation as mean(row_energy/row_mass), avoiding final dense row scaling. Matches existing exact row balancing within rounding.357 linearizes reciprocal only on reduced rowenergy.358-360 combine this with class simplifications/mass checks. All30 pass.359 worstnRMSE0.00705296 versus3440.00912993 and received full official evaluation.\n-366/367 remove unreachable initial-full-kernel branches after mean initialization and redundant setup.368/369 replace second dense column/row factors with exact rowenergy/mass matrix-vector contractions (Torch/NumPy). See index for final local/official evidence; these are controlled implementation ablations, not automatically retained.\n\n## Artifacts and reproducibility\nEpisode6 preserved **104 source snapshots**, **103 complete local screens**, **79 all30 local-valid variants**, **24 numerical-failure variants**, and **5 complete official evaluations**. `episode_6_index.json`, `episode6_official_summary.json`, and `episode_6_final_selection.json` summarize them. Builders, fresh-process timing logs, source files and unchanged official reports remain under `/tmp/research_history`. `episode_6_working_notes.md` records chronology. One hard-only builder syntax error was caught before executing anycase; original invalid builder and initial sources are preserved before the correction.\n\n`screen_all.py` compares against outputs previously computed from saved canonical initial implementation using only publicarrays; those caches are local research artifacts and never used inside submitted method. Local screens are not evaluator scores. Fresh benchmarkround5 had unusually large externalhost timingnoise, preserved in its rawtimelists. Official evaluations had no concurrent own numerical workloads.\n\n## Continuation\nStart from exact selected source and profile before extending. General sparseclass graphs, full segmented NumPy, Gaussian conditional point approximations, grouped point solves, and shared geometry were extensively explored this episode; repeating them without a materially new mechanism is unlikely useful. Exact rowenergy reduction is mathematically sound; whether it wins depends on kernel layout and reductioncost. Keep finite canonical annealing conventions: solving to convergence or removing all coarse class soft transforms changes the operator. Improvements must pass all30 gates and complete real `solve.sh` protocol before retention.\n\nNo protected assets or frozen launcher changed. No protected targets, private inputs, evaluator source, other trajectories, credentials, environment variables, network, external package installation or subagents accessed. Submission has exactly method.py and frozen solve.sh as root files; generated method cache removed. No evaluation pending.\n\nFinal diagnostic profile on public303_gaussian016:0.0754478seconds including profiling overhead, class transport0.033, point duals0.025, coupling0.007. This is not an evaluator score. Final source hash and frozen launcher hash verified, root submission files exactly method.py and solve.sh, and no evaluation pending. The selected source improves historical best time by2.9017percent and the episode6 start-source repeat by10.3279percent; compare full protocol results because timing noise is substantial.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
