{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc 'cat /tmp/research_history/episode_1_working_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_2_continuing_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_3_continuing_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": "# Hierarchical transport research, episode 1\n\nRead /workspace/instruction.md, method.py, solve.sh and all public metadata/NPZ array schemas. No earlier history existed. Submission consists solely of method.py and frozen solve.sh. No protected targets or evaluator code inspected.\n\nLauncher is read-only mode 0444, so ./solve.sh directly fails permission denied. Invoke `sh ./solve.sh` (exact same unchanged script). Launcher SHA256: bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1.\n\nBaseline source copied to /tmp/research_history/000_baseline/method.py, SHA256 92b12caf57128aac8eb7ca32c0e9e3d6f0fb45ec479b78a8243c093922bc5993. Full evaluator started; log /tmp/research_history/000_baseline/evaluator.log. Results pending.\n\nCanonical: W computes 190 pairwise class OT values but value uses ONLY W[:10,10:20] (100 costs). Main batches contiguous 1024 (N=3000 gives 3; M=2000 gives 2). Each pair uses cost C=||a-b||²/2+W/2. Batch expectation uses POT reg .1 on C/C.max(). Point potentials use GeomLoss blur sqrt(.1), debias=False, p=2, with symmetric epsilon scaling .5 and final extrapolation; potentials centered and scaled n/(n-1). Outer Q uses POT reg .01 on batchcost/batchcost.max().\n\nHypotheses queued:\n1. Eliminate unused within-candidate and within-reference W solves, maintaining exact cross costs.\n2. Replace GeomLoss dense implementation with precomputed symmetric costs, matching its finite epsilon annealing schedule. Potential acceleration: at very low epsilon, logsumexp is effectively hard min, sparse top-k or late-stage min transforms. Need ablate carefully as targets are the finite-iteration canonical algorithm rather than exact converged OT.\n3. Vectorize class-conditional solves with padding and masks / grouped same schedules.\n4. Separate batch coupling expectation from potentials; high-reg normalized C may allow approximate couplings or low-rank kernels, subject to strict value gates.\n\nResearch records must preserve all tried variants (including failures) and exact evaluator-reported source hashes. Every retained candidate needs full actual Public/Dev evaluation.\n\nPrepared 001_cross_only (not yet evaluated): canonical with only 10x10 cross-class W solves. Prepared 002_reuse_cost (not yet evaluated): manual GeomLoss-equivalent epsilon schedule and symmetric dual updates, reuse C transpose rather than recomputing reverse geometry. Will measure exact reuse before approximations.\n\nPrepared 003_late_min (not yet evaluated): based on 002, replace logsumexp softmin with hard minimum only when epsilon <=2, retaining the epsilon*log(support size) offset. Public input distance quantiles confirm scales of hundreds/thousands; this is a controlled low-temperature approximation. Preserve all variants, run full evaluation for any candidate retained.\n\nBaseline full evaluator completed: status ok, quality_valid true, raw_metric 0.7168641031021252, score -0.37463374328318144, exact reported source hash 92b12caf57128aac8eb7ca32c0e9e3d6f0fb45ec479b78a8243c093922bc5993. Result copied to 000_baseline/result.json. The protected formal baseline differs from this noisy agent run (score implies B~0.52149). Full 001_cross_only evaluation now running, method.py currently 001.\n\nLocal screen script compares methods against outputs computed from saved canonical source on allowed public arrays (not protected targets); all logs in research_history. Local fresh invocation timings noisy and include thread setup. Results: 001 exactly canonical; 002 normalized RMSE <=4.6e-7. 003/004/005 nrmse <=0.000751 across six seed-101 cases, Spearman >=0.99999922 and overlap >=0.9986667. 004 batches label costs; 005 replaces POT batch-level expectation by torch float32 scaling and reuses SciPy geometry also for potentials. 005 not clearly faster locally. 006/007/008 test hard-min transforms for both class geometry and point potentials at eps <=8/32/128 respectively; screen pending. They are explicit ablations of a numerical approximation, not yet retained or formally validated.\n\nLocal ablations 006/007/008 completed. 006 eps<=8 hard minima in W and point potentials: seed-101 max nrmse 0.00491525, min rho 0.9999864, min top overlap 0.9973333; clear local runtime ~.21-.28s in non-overlapping portion. 007 threshold32 fails nrmse in every seed-101 case (max .03124); 008 threshold128 fails nrmse, rank, overlap (max .13264). These failures are saved, never submit them.\n\n009_gibbs_early uses explicit exponential kernels and matvec for early epsilon stages with dynamic range <60, log-domain fallback for remaining stages, hard min <=8. Quality numerically identical to 006. Timings screen009 are not directly comparable (concurrent evaluator). 010_sparse_late replaces hard min for point potentials with a fixed 32-neighbor graph constructed from current dual reduced costs when eps<=32, retaining entropy on the graph and matching finite symmetric update schedule. W remains batched hybrid min8. Screening pending.\n\n010/011 sparse32 at eps<=32 pass local six-case screens with max nrmse ~0.001395 and min rank ~0.9999982. 011 also sparsifies batched class costs, reducing their entropy truncation error. 012 k8 at same early graph entry fails nrmse (max .0146955); 013 k4 fails nrmse (max .031154). Graph pruning can miss later changing nearest neighbors because canonical is finite schedule, not converged OT. 014 k16/entry32, 015 k8/entry8, 016 k4/entry2 are later/more conservative graph ablations; screen pending. Official 001 still running. Next full evaluations planned: 006 conservative hard-min and best promising graph method. All method variants and local logs archived; no screen-only source is yet the submitted best.\n\n001_cross_only full evaluation completed, quality valid all30: raw .5246864919996975, score -.0061206209469174, exact reported method_sha256 f855f22591fb86743419256f78afae10b9b36baa4f4f3de50d4df270a38fe504. Result saved 001_cross_only/result.json. This is current best formally validated source (method.py already same).\n\nAll30 local canonical screens completed for 006/014/015. 006 max nrmse .0057234561, min rho .9999783404, min overlap .9933333; 014 max nrmse .0065429912, min rho .9999810547, min overlap .992; 015 max nrmse .0022599875, min rho .9999959627, min overlap .9973333. All locally valid, but full evaluator required. 015 looks safest graph method, sparsifies to8 neighbors only when epsilon<=8 (so truncation depends on duals near completion).\n\n017_fused_reductions (not evaluated): use -(eps*logsumexp((h-C)/eps)+eps*log(weight)) to avoid redundant scalar addition/divisions on dense matrices, based on006. Algebraically same except floating point rounding. Fair local bench_round_1 comparing6,9,10,11,14,15,16,17 started after evaluator and screen processes completed. Fresh same-input repeated timings are screening only.\n\nFair one-input local bench after001 completion: median006 .20375s, 009 .18108s, 010 .20966s, 011 .22746s, 014 .20217s, 015 .20565s, 016 .21072s, 017 .21396s. Favor009 for full evaluator; full009 started with archived source copied to method.py. Current validated best remains001 until full009 completes.\n\n018_batched_gibbs extends009's early-stage exponential kernel + matrix-vector approach to the 100 batched class solves, preserving hybrid min8 late stage and log-domain fallback. All30 local screen valid; max nrmse .005723617, min rho .999978327, min top .9933333 (essentially006). 019_sparse_min prunes low-temperature hard-min point updates to8 neighbors selected at eps<=8; class solver unchanged009. Testing whether pruning reduces full dense minima while respecting canonical finite schedule; screen pending. One public-input profile009 indicates residual time split among point dual solves, normalized coupling expectations, and label solve. Contended local profile not used for official aggregate.\n\n019 sparse hard-min graph all30 locally valid, max nrmse .0057814064, min rho .9999776991, min overlap .9933333. 020_shared_reduced_cost shares the matrix C-f-g for row and column minima (exact algebra of hard-min update), based018; all30 local valid max nrmse .0057236426, min rank .9999783271, min top .9933333. 021_cached_kernels caches repeated initial-temperature kernels and precomputes centered costs for every dense Gibbs stage; based020, all kernels are per-call derived from current arrays, no cross-input cache. Screen pending. Aim is to reduce exponentiation and memory traffic without additional numerical approximation.\n\n009 full evaluated: status ok, quality_valid true, raw .2715824751324059, score .4792228641581371, exact source hash ea63f829bbdba097fc488d3a5115108125e09949410e77f09cc2151420a43ba5. All270 outputs quality extrema nrmse .0057236062, rho .9999783271, overlap .9933333. Saved result009. This is best fully evaluated so far. 015 full evaluation running.\n\n021 all30 local valid with essentially same quality as018/020. Fair bench_round_2 completed: 009 median .25461 (noisy initial), 015 .21803, 018 .17182, 019 .17413, 020 .17494, 021 .16999. 021 gains are small; full formal evaluation will decide.\n\n022_initialize_threads moves only thread-count configuration to source initialization (fixed non-input configuration; all geometry remains within value), based021. Prepared to understand fresh-call thread setup overhead, no formal result yet. Frozen launcher unchanged throughout.\n\n023/024/025/026 controlled batch-coupling ablations based021: normalized Gibbs kernel still exact; cap scaling at3/1/2/5 updates respectively and retain row-corrected expectation. All30 local screens: 023 max nrmse .0057770232, min rank .9999779964, min top .9933333, valid; 024 fails8 cases on RMSE (max .0123598761), archived and reject; 025 max nrmse .0061514938, min rank .9999742760, min top .992, valid; 026 max nrmse .0057227272, min rank .9999783329, min top .9933333, valid. Clear batch-coupling approximations yield their own error rather than assuming canonical marginal matrices must match. 027/028 adapt convergence checks every iteration (reuse K.T @ u instead of repeating it) with norm tolerance1e-6/1e-5, cap30. All included geometry remains data-derived and timed.\n\n015 full evaluation quality valid, but slower than009: raw .3041770834014945, score .41672057372136356, exact source hash 494783a939f65bbd7cd6654e3cfb44ae0d7c26028aa05dca54fb3179b543389e. Quality extrema nrmse .00225998745, rank .99999596267, overlap .9973333. Archived complete report result015. Reverted method.py to009 immediately after this worse aggregate. The graph method provides extra accuracy but does not improve speed enough. Bench_round_3 comparing21/22/23/25/26/27/28 runs without evaluator concurrency.\n\n027 and028 all30 local screens valid; max RMSE ~.0057230 and .0057227 respectively, min rho .99997830/.99997833, min overlap .9933333. They adapt every-step checks and avoid redundant K.T @ u; possible more robust alternatives to fixed3 inner scaling if comparable time.\n\n023 full evaluation is running. Bench_round_3 was unusually slow throughout (roughly2x previous round), indicating host/runtime variance; still fixed3 expectation generally faster than convergence-controlled alternatives. It is the next formal candidate, all30 locally valid. Keep009 if it reports a worse aggregate.\n\n029_one_thread based023 configures torch1 thread (at most2 cores allowed), paired local test versus023 while evaluation running: .3982s vs .4935s median (contention makes absolute times unsuitable; alternating order controls drift). 030/031_torch_geometry reuse torch.cdist geometry for both coupling and point potentials, based023/029 (2/1 torch threads). All30 local screens valid max nrmse .0057770750, min rho .9999779964, min overlap .9933333. This removes repeated double SciPy distance geometry and float32 copy.\n\n032_numpy_dual based031 replaces ONLY point annealing updates by NumPy arrays, SciPy logsumexp, preserving cached Gibbs kernels, shared reduced-cost minima and symmetric finite schedule. This is a library/kernel implementation ablation, not a learned surrogate. Local screen pending. All sources and logs saved. No newly screened source is retained as final until full evaluation.\n\n023 full completed quality valid; raw .2590207737681339, score .503310747054841, exact reported source hash fb46e5d3b5dc019ba7d3858876ad64af9a951d943f9e5d0229b19707e0e99ad0. Quality max nrmse .0057769853, min rho .9999779964, min top .9933333. Full result archived023/result.json. This is current best, method.py remains023. Seed aggregates101 .23734,202 .32743,303 .28082,404 .23621,505 .21330 seconds; variability substantial.\n\n032 all30 local valid max nrmse .0057772001, min rank .9999780027, min top .9933333. 033_numpy_labels extends032 by moving masked batched class dual solves to NumPy/SciPy too; keeps Torch geometry/coupling. All30 local valid max nrmse .0057772199, min rank .9999780036, min top .9933333. Library implementations algebraically mirror the hybrid approximation; class geometry remains computed from inputs. Paired alternating bench_round_4 comparing023/029/030/031/032/033 without evaluator concurrency. Next fully evaluate fastest kernel implementation, keep only if aggregate improves.\n\nClean alternating bench_round_4 (no evaluator running) overturned the contended one-thread result: medians023 .21498,029 .25002,030 .18192,031 .25223,032 .39500,033 .38525 seconds. Use2 Torch threads. NumPy/SciPy general logsumexp is slower. Full030 evaluation started (Torch geometry reused, based023). 034_numpy_inplace is a last kernel ablation: based032 but2 Torch threads, explicit in-place stable float32 logsumexp, clamp centered logits below-80 (numerical contributions <1e-31 total). Avoids general SciPy logsumexp overhead and denormal exponents. Local screen pending. If it is clearly faster in local clean benchmark, fully evaluate it; otherwise retain best full result030 or023.\n# Continuing research notes: episode 2 complete\n\n## State and rules\n- Only `/workspace/solution/method.py` is editable submission. Frozen launcher unchanged SHA256 `bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1`; mode0444, invoke `sh ./solve.sh`.\n- No protected targets, evaluator source, private inputs, credentials, environment variables, other trajectories, network or agents accessed. Public inputs plus locally computed canonical outputs only. Official reports are copied unchanged from paths printed by our own evaluator.\n- **Current best fully evaluated/restored:075_scalar_outer**, raw .197422715400171, score .6214290475624421, all30 quality-valid. Exact evaluator hash `1c8792d45354197af5834a1813b6f7fe554f418b811e07620270a678bc6b54fb`. Worst nRMSE .0031568536690847943, min Spearman .9999905964433994, min overlap .996.\n- No evaluation pending. Full079_numpy_coupling passed all30 but raw .2315128375320152, score .5560590116068239 was worse than075. Exact reported hash `f406f42ecbad9d58e480f5b6b9452f00ccbe4a46148cd024c24fea4488d0c377`. Report copied unchanged to079/result.json;075 remains restored.\n- History: snapshots, full logs and official result.json under `/tmp/research_history`. Full chronological notes `episode_2_working_notes.md`; summary `episode_2_index.json` can be regenerated with `make_episode2_index.py`. Prior concise notes preserved in `episode_1_continuing_notes.md`. No reports altered.\n\n## Formal results\n| Variant | Raw seconds | Score | Quality | Exact evaluator hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|\n|030_torch_geometry (episode1 best)|.20901791953365317|.599194486237406|all30 valid|b92818cc1bb2e79042a1d59c7bf57eba0f5bc18597b8d94c8ed3e1fe82230e73|\n|043_shared_exponential_all|.5545745612675091|-.06343294605753307|all30 valid|d5216ab4213ef2eed92d173dab42d30d33e2e56c045b23dc1adac67b484a47e6|\n|075_scalar_outer|.197422715400171|.6214290475624421|all30 valid|1c8792d45354197af5834a1813b6f7fe554f418b811e07620270a678bc6b54fb|\n|079_numpy_coupling|.2315128375320152|.5560590116068239|all30 valid|f406f42ecbad9d58e480f5b6b9452f00ccbe4a46148cd024c24fea4488d0c377|\n\n043 ran with local screens concurrently, and host timing regime was much slower than episode1.075 ran alone. Keep official numerical outcomes/time distinct from local comparisons and avoid concurrent numerical work during formal timing. Earlier6 full reports listed in episode1 notes; none are faster than075.\n\n## Current numerical recipe\n- Canonical contiguous1024 batches, cross100 empirical class costs from first candidate/reference batches. Cost C=||x-r||²/2+W[y,ry]/2. Torch cdist float32 geometry reused for point potentials and normalized coupling.\n- Shared residual R=C-f-g yields both soft c-transforms from one exp and row/column sums. Shift by global minimum, clamp exponent below-80 to avoid denormals, fallback stable logsumexp if a support sum <=2e-22. Missing tail then below1e-9 relative.075 uses Torch in-place;079 NumPy in-place (same computation).\n- Skip the first2 duplicate coarse symmetric updates for both class/point solves, retain initial c-transform from zero. Skip final3 symmetric point updates; final candidate c-transform still at epsilon.1. Point hard-min only <=2, class hard-min when all eps<=8. Finite canonical schedule is important; full convergence is not target.\n- Normalized coupling epsilon=.1 uses3 alternating scaling updates then row-corrected expectation. Outer entropy reg=.01 after max-normalization. For2 reference batches, scalar safeguarded Newton finds logistic probabilities satisfying column mass.5; arbitrary reference batch counts use canonical POT.\n-075 Torch coupling;079 pure NumPy dense elementwise scaling reductions, no BLAS configuration/process operations. All values derive from supplied arrays, no lookup/training/saved coefficients. No per-input caches across invocations.\n\n## Episode2 ablations and decisions\n-035/036/037 truncate1/2/3 late point updates: all30 local valid, worst nRMSE .005784/.005836/.006206.044 truncate4 fails5,045 truncate5 fails30. Keep3;070 even with soft threshold2 truncate4 fails16, max .01321.\n-038/039 union of row/column argmin active edges at eps8 fails29/30.040 union2 neighbors fails4.051/052 enter graph later at eps2: all30 valid, max .004872/.004260, but no clear speed win. Do not confuse fixed sparse support with exact c-transform.\n-041 shared exponent only middle stages,042 also labels,043 almost all temperatures; all30 local valid ~.005777.046 direct squared matmul geometry and047 candidate-only final update valid.048/049/050 combine shared exponent/direct geometry/truncation/no transpose; all valid, geometry branch sometimes unexpectedly slower.\n-053/054 batch point solves in groups2/6 with masks: all30 numerically same but no clear speed gain.055/056 single thread did not clearly beat2; prior episode2 threads were faster in clean comparisons.\n-057/058 skip1/2 duplicate coarse point updates valid;059 also class duplicate2 valid.060 combines coarse2/late3 valid max .006306.061 in-place shared exp/strided cost transpose;062 adds short schedule; valid.063 removes coupling transpose copy;064 also2 coupling updates;065 short schedule+2-step coupling; valid but not faster consistently.\n-066/067 skip3 coarse point updates with0/3 late removals valid max .008918/.009181, little speed win and less margin.068 skip4 fails all30 max .06248.069 uses soft minima down to2 with coarse2/late3, valid max .00315685 and substantially better accuracy.071 skips coarse3 with soft2 valid max .006737.\n-072 only2 inner coupling updates: valid max .003963.073 coupling1 fails6 max .011721. No useful further gain identified for1 step.\n-075 scalar outer equivalent to069 on all30. Random stress tests showed scalar column residual <6.4e-14; occasional generic POT unconverged stress examples differ, but public outputs match to rounding. Full075 valid and new best.\n-076 pure NumPy shared point transforms;077 also class transforms;078 NumPy point+coupling;079 all3;080/081 scratch buffers. All30 local valid max .00315698. Clean alternating round4 medians075 .34325,076 .23944,077 .21913,078 .24411,079 .19722,080 .20458,081 .24826. NumPy shared approach is faster than prior episode NumPy double-logsumexp approach.079 selected for full report but formal timing was worse than075; reject for retention. Scratch buffer reuse did not improve time.\n-082 Gaussian class W2 from empirical means/covariances fails all30 max .04067.083 Gaussian + empirical separable correction from19 anchor pairs fails21 max .03268.084 Gaussian+CUR residual from36 anchor pairs fails27 max .41626. These compressions do not preserve required empirical geometry; reject.\n\n## Local tools/evidence\n- `screen_all.py` uses public NPZ arrays and cached outputs computed by our saved canonical source (not protected targets). Reports all30 gates. `paired_bench.py` alternates source order8 rounds on seed303_gaussian016; not formal score.\n- Clean round3 medians030 .63708,062 .30763,069 .33459,072 .31470,075 .33108. Runtime host noise substantial; full official075 .19742. Never infer official score from local speed.\n- Suggested continuation if needed: start from formal winner075; optimize cost/memory passes with paired tests, consider input-derived entropy tail truncation without losing finite-schedule behavior. Gaussian geometry and sparse active graphs are rejected. Preserve failed attempts and evaluate every candidate to retain.\n\nEpisode2 complete after49 archived new sources, numerous all30 local ablations,4 clean/controlled timing rounds and3 complete official evaluations. New best075 improves measured raw over030 by .0115952041 seconds (5.55%) and improves accuracy margin. Exact best source and frozen launcher hashes verified, submission has only method.py and solve.sh. No protected assets changed.\n# Continuing research notes: episode 3 complete\n\n## Best fully evaluated state\n- Best is123_flush_reciprocal_relative_check, raw **.07845975276820051**, score **.8495482990735561**, all30 quality-valid. Exact evaluator source hash **011e216f7713a4e107d99beedc0a24c769c46e7b53a7e657463191a50d6ac4cc**. Worst nRMSE .003961464539171208, minrho .9999831568870172, mintop .996.\n- **Current method.py restored to exact best123. No evaluation pending.**125 fourth official run passed all30, raw .08285442496902154, score .841121228068361, slower than123; rejected. Exact reported source hash9a2123b1f10334586e1bbf44d6ef8138df7d753cd13de6f46c7276fce277cb63. Report copied unchanged. Final hashes and exactly two root submission files verified; protected infrastructure folders untouched.\n- Frozen solve.sh unchanged SHA256 bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1, mode0444; use `sh ./solve.sh`.\n- Only public inputs/local canonical computations used. No protected targets/evaluator source/private inputs, credentials, environment variables, network or agents accessed. No protected assets edited. Submission contains exactly method.py and frozen solve.sh; our generated workspace __pycache__ removed.\n\n## Formal results in this episode\n| Variant | Raw seconds | Score | Quality | Exact evaluator hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|\n|086_recurrent_torch_kernel|.10838529869943159|.7921641100128568|all30 valid|6a1459c74bce4a5c80d0972b2627033fe8c8a6edced4c0c36c6c30f4f0c363b4|\n|113_remove_redundant_reductions|.15102290423431744|.7104037163100325|all30 valid, slower|99714c8a2394f719327815a13c559ffcead26c03c584a6c7d605efa2a93285e1|\n|123_flush_reciprocal_relative_check|.07845975276820051|.8495482990735561|all30 valid, best|011e216f7713a4e107d99beedc0a24c769c46e7b53a7e657463191a50d6ac4cc|\n|125_numpy_coupling_reductions|.08285442496902154|.841121228068361|all30 valid, slower|9a2123b1f10334586e1bbf44d6ef8138df7d753cd13